  "knowledgePattern": "Recover sine and cosine together, then sum the common angular progression",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Treat sine and cosine as two unknowns",
      "body": "<p>Add the two equations to eliminate the cosine term. Then substitute back to find the cosine value too.</p>",
      "recap": "A simultaneous solution must satisfy both equations.",
      "pitfall": "Do not solve only one of the two trigonometric equations.",
      "pause": "Find both trig values.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-simultaneous-equations",
          "reminder": "A simultaneous solution must satisfy both equations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Find the repeating angles",
      "body": "<p>Find an angle whose sine and cosine both have those values. Add whole turns to the angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, then divide to obtain the allowed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> values.</p>",
      "recap": "The period belongs to the whole trigonometric input.",
      "pitfall": "These angles are measured in degrees.",
      "pause": "List the nonnegative x values in increasing order.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l15",
          "reminder": "The period belongs to the whole trigonometric input."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Add the values in order",
      "body": "<p>Keep a running sum of your list until it reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>800</mn></mrow></math>. The least upper limit must include the last value used.</p>",
      "recap": "An interval includes exactly the sequence terms at or below its upper endpoint.",
      "pitfall": "The question asks for the least k, not for the sum again.",
      "pause": "Identify the smallest endpoint that includes the required terms.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l08",
          "reminder": "Identify the first term, last term and exact number of terms in the arithmetic block."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Adding the equations gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>sin</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Substituting into the first gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Both hold when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p>The nonnegative solutions begin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>140</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>260</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>380</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Their running sums are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>160</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>420</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>800</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Every later solution is positive, so the required sum uses exactly these four.</p><p>The least upper limit including all four is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>380</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, which is C.</p>"
